WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.728
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.227 --> 00:00:05.790
SAM MCVEETY: Welcome.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:09.410
Today you'll be hearing about
Nest's use of Cloud Platform

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:12.500
to prototype massive
scale services

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.380
and using a philosophy
I affectionately refer

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.710
to as launching and iterating.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.750
So I'm Sam McVeety.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.970
I'm the tech lead
of Cloud Dataflow.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.120
And I'm really thrilled to be
joined today by Kevin and Peter

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.590
from Nest.

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.660
And most of the
talk today is going

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.036
to be their show, because we
just built the infrastructure.

00:00:33.036 --> 00:00:34.160
They did all the hard work.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.370
So I'll give most
of the time to them.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:41.180
But before then, by
way of an introduction,

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:44.090
I'll talk briefly about
what launching and iterating

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.060
looks like on Google's cloud.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.580
And then they'll go into
a little bit more depth

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.770
on what specifically
Nest is doing

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:55.380
and how they're
building their services.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.110
So launching and iterating--

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.870
one of the things I really
love about working at Google

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.900
is this idea that
you can take an idea

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.180
and you can prototype it.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:11.250
You can see what works, what
doesn't, tweak it, fail fast,

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.240
succeed quickly if you're lucky.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:14.400
But you can do that.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.400
And you can quickly
make progress that way.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.214
And you're going to see how
the Nest folks did that.

00:01:20.214 --> 00:01:21.630
And what I love
about the cloud is

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.480
that it brings this
capability to everyone.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.410
It's not something that
is locked in at Google.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.760
It is something that anybody
that uses Google Cloud

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.500
has access to.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.490
So you sort of take one thing
away from this presentation,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.240
it's going to be--

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.240
I hope it's this quote.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.810
One engineer, no systems
background, and three days.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.330
And this isn't an outlier.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:49.110
This is how you can quickly use
the cloud to validate ideas,

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:55.180
to move forward, make
progress, succeed quickly.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.492
For example, we had a hackathon
recently on the Dataflow team.

00:01:58.492 --> 00:02:00.950
And this is what it looked like
where, in a matter of days,

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.430
people were building
things on infrastructure

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.710
and turning those ideas
into a reality, to say, hey,

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:11.260
this is something we want to run
with or eh, maybe not so good.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.800
So in making these
things a reality,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.090
let's talk briefly about some
of the tech that's available.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:21.120
So this first one
is Google BigQuery.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.060
So this is your fast,
economical data warehouse

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.242
for large scale analytics.

00:02:27.242 --> 00:02:29.200
And you can really throw
all your data in here.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.610
You don't have to worry
about indices or sharding.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.740
You just send all
your data to BigQuery.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.940
You can you use
SQL, standard SQL,

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.154
to ask questions about the data.

00:02:39.154 --> 00:02:41.320
And you don't have to know
what those questions look

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:42.250
like upfront.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.856
You're not doing any
sort of preoptimization.

00:02:44.856 --> 00:02:47.230
So if it turns out that you're
asking the wrong questions

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:48.771
or you didn't know
what questions you

00:02:48.771 --> 00:02:52.030
were going to ask, it's
there to iterate on.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:56.720
And there's no backtracking,
no repartitioning necessary.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.110
Now to get the data
there in the first place,

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.960
you need something
like App Engine.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.770
So this allows you to build
a scalable web application

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:09.490
quickly on any of the languages
that Google's infrastructure

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:10.840
supports.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.800
You can stand up
a service simply,

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.680
throw up some endpoints.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.840
And if we're talking about
devices that you just

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:21.070
want to send simple REST calls
from, like for the Nest folks,

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:22.900
this is a great way
for them to phone home,

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.460
send telemetry data quickly.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:28.307
And then you can
analyze that offline.

00:03:28.307 --> 00:03:30.140
Now if you want something
a little more full

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:34.250
featured than SQL, that's were
something like Cloud Dataflow

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:35.690
comes in.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:39.320
So using the Apache Beam
model, Cloud Dataflow

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.900
provides a way to
write your code

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.330
and run it in batch
or streaming mode.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:47.300
So upfront, you don't know
if you care about latency.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.570
You can iterate on your code
while it's in batch mode.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:53.780
And then if latency turns out
to be something you care about,

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:57.560
you can run it in a
stream processing fashion.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.020
Also you don't have to
worry about provisioning.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:01.430
It's a managed service.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:02.480
It has autoscaling.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.910
So you're not worrying
about replicas upfront.

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:06.440
You just say, hey,
here's my code.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:07.590
I want to run it.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.430
I want to see how it works.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:15.130
And then sort of stepping
back a level, in general,

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:16.880
you don't even have
to know which cloud do

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.510
you want to run on, at first.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:21.760
You want to prototype
on Google's cloud.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.320
Our commitment to
open source means

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:26.010
that if it turns out
the investment isn't

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:29.710
the right place, you can move,
because you're not locked in.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:31.600
So with BigQuery
for example, you

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.460
have standards SQL
support where you can

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:35.310
run those queries elsewhere.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.330
For App Engine, you
can take your container

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.320
and run it on Kubernetes.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:43.450
And for Dataflow, you have
this engine portability by Beam

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.850
where you can run that
on the Dataflow runner.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:48.850
You can run that
on Spark, anything

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.280
that implements the standard.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.994
So altogether, this
provides this ability

00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:55.910
to launch and iterate.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:59.029
You don't have to answer all
these questions, provisioning

00:04:59.029 --> 00:05:00.070
all these things upfront.

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:01.930
You just have to have an idea.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.090
And you can validate
that idea quickly

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:06.790
without letting all these
details get in the way.

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:09.152
So, thank you again
for coming, and let

00:05:09.152 --> 00:05:10.360
me introduce Kevin from Nest.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:14.880
KEVIN PETERSON: Thanks Sam.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.242
So I want to start here
just by introducing

00:05:17.242 --> 00:05:19.200
Nest and a little bit
about what our mission is

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.658
and what we're
trying to accomplish

00:05:20.658 --> 00:05:22.710
and how we're going to
use the GCP infrastructure

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:24.330
that Sam's team has worked on.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:25.830
So at Nest, our
mission is to create

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.750
a home that takes care
of the people inside it

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.540
and the world around it.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:32.160
And we accomplish this mission
via the different products

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:33.330
that we have.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.242
So we take unloved
products in homes

00:05:35.242 --> 00:05:37.200
and try to reinvent them
and make them smarter,

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.240
make them do more for you.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:41.160
So we started with the
Learning Thermostat,

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.880
followed that up with the
smoke and CO alarm, and then

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:45.390
an indoor and outdoor camera.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:46.890
But more than just
the products, we

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:49.056
have the app that's behind
it that lets you actually

00:05:49.056 --> 00:05:52.260
interface with your
devices and your home,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.660
and then that service which
sort of backs everything up.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.390
So Nest is sort of often
considered a hardware company,

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:59.850
but are truly much more
than that because the apps

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:01.920
and the services that
are behind everything

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.280
are really the important bits.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.247
So I'm part of the
algorithms team at Nest,

00:06:08.247 --> 00:06:10.080
and I'm going to introduce
a little bit what

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.210
my team is responsible for.

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:17.520
So we are the team that sort
of takes the data from--

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.370
takes our data and tries to
make intelligent features

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:21.294
for your devices.

00:06:21.294 --> 00:06:22.710
And so we have a
principal at Nest

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.501
that any data that's
measured by our device

00:06:24.501 --> 00:06:27.300
should return value to
you, the user of it.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.060
And we do this via features
like Home/Away Assist and auto

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:30.870
schedule.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.580
We're trying to make sure
that when you're not home

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:34.954
or when you don't want your
home to be heating or cooling

00:06:34.954 --> 00:06:37.290
itself, we can turn off
your AC or your heating

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.330
and make sure that
you save energy.

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:42.900
We also use our sensors to build
thermal models of the home.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.200
And this lets us offer
features like Time-to-Temp,

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.820
so that when you change the
temperature of your home,

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:49.350
we tell you how long it's
going to take to get there

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.330
so you know, maybe you're
leaving in an hour,

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.080
you don't need to turn
it to a temperature

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:55.110
that it's not going to
reach for two hours.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:57.090
Rush Hour Rewards is a
partnership with energy

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:59.070
services and energy programs.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:01.830
This lets us actually
precool your home

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.560
before maybe a time
of day when energy's

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.220
going to be very expensive.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:09.630
And this will let you save
money plus let the energy

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:12.060
companies avoid these
really high peak loads that

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:14.040
can cause rolling blackouts.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.290
Steam Check and Sunblock
are both features

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.240
that fuse multiple
sensors on the device

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.399
to make the device
smarter and do more.

00:07:20.399 --> 00:07:22.440
So with Steam Check, we
can detect the difference

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.410
between steam and smoke
and actually build

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.180
a smoke alarm that
is less annoying, not

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.550
going to go off at
7:00 in the morning

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:32.250
when you get on the
shower, wake the kids up.

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:34.010
And then with Sunblock,
we can detect,

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:36.282
is the temperature in the
home actually going up,

00:07:36.282 --> 00:07:38.490
or is it just the sun has
come through the window now

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:40.200
and is hitting the
thermostat and we

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:42.690
see a rise in temperature,
but your home is still

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.273
the same temperature
and we don't need

00:07:44.273 --> 00:07:47.060
to waste energy cooling it.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.136
So, my team is
responsible for taking

00:07:49.136 --> 00:07:51.260
these features all the way
from the very beginning,

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:55.170
from maybe an idea, a sheet
of paper in your lab notebook,

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.177
through the production scale.

00:07:57.177 --> 00:07:58.760
And so what I want
to talk about today

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:01.370
is how we use GCP to
really take these concepts

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:03.290
from the notebook
and show that they're

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:04.850
feasible and worth investing in.

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:07.369
So I'm sort of at this
very beginning stage.

00:08:07.369 --> 00:08:09.410
We're still trying to find
out, is this possible?

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:11.105
Is it something we can do?

00:08:11.105 --> 00:08:13.730
And so we don't want to invest
a ton of the company's resources

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:17.086
on an idea that we haven't
even shown is doable yet.

00:08:17.086 --> 00:08:19.460
And so the first thing we need
to do is build a prototype

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:21.600
and collect data.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.940
And so at this scale,
or at this stage,

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:26.540
we're sort of sitting at a
lab bench, maybe have a sensor

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:28.920
that we've ordered
from the manufacturer,

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:30.650
or trying to figure
out, can we measure

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:32.022
the things we need to measure?

00:08:32.022 --> 00:08:34.480
And we found that each engineer
had a very different method

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:36.146
that they like to run
their experiments,

00:08:36.146 --> 00:08:40.350
that they like to store
and organize their data.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.860
Some people would use
Google Spreadsheet

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:45.410
to keep track of all the
experiments they're running.

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:47.900
Other people would actually
embed the different parameters

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:51.650
in the filename itself so
they could parse it out later.

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:53.660
And this just
didn't really scale.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:56.240
We weren't able to
communicate very well what

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:57.776
each different
engineer was doing.

00:08:57.776 --> 00:08:59.150
And it was hard
to make that data

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:01.910
available to the people
that needed to see to make

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:04.550
their decisions early on.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:08.810
And so we were sort of
faced with this problem of,

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:12.270
how do we collect and label
data in a scalable way?

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:16.250
And so we need to take data
that's sitting on sensors,

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:18.320
probably plugged into a
laptop, maybe a Raspberry

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:21.230
Pi or something like that, and
get it to the engineers that

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:22.250
are going to use it.

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:23.840
And because this
is GCP Next, you

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:27.300
can probably guess how I'm
going to go about this.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.450
So, we want to
build an App Engine

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:33.500
project to take that data,
put it in a few data stores,

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:36.166
and then feed it back
to the engineers.

00:09:36.166 --> 00:09:37.790
And so we built a
service an App Engine

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:40.371
that allows ingest
and data querying.

00:09:40.371 --> 00:09:42.620
We used Cloud Storage, which
is Google's object store,

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:44.430
to store the blobs
of sensor data.

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:48.290
So this is temperature
samples, humidity, smoke,

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:53.960
things like that that are
really just CSVs or JSON blobs.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:56.690
And then the important bit
here is using Cloud Datastore

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:58.630
as a metadata service.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:00.380
And so here we can
actually label our data

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:03.650
in a sort of scalable way, a way
that everybody can understand,

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:05.490
and we can query that
to get the data back.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:07.490
So you can say, give me
all the experiments that

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:09.190
were run at room temperature.

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:12.050
Give me all the experiments that
were run at 50 degrees C. So

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:14.480
we can make sure that as
we develop our products,

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.330
we know exactly how
they're working.

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:19.765
And the first thing we found
in building the service

00:10:19.765 --> 00:10:21.660
is that people wanted to use it.

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:23.510
And so now we have
the same data being

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:26.312
used by the hardware engineers
to validate their sensors.

00:10:26.312 --> 00:10:28.520
We have the same data being
used by our embedded team

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:30.650
as they're sort of trying
to prototype and take

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:34.665
these algorithms and
put them on the devices.

00:10:34.665 --> 00:10:37.290
And what's more is this data was
immediately available to them.

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:40.850
So as soon as people collect
the data in the lab, now

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:43.430
they can immediately query it
sitting at their desk running

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:46.510
simulations, things like that.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:48.820
And then we can scale
it up even further

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:51.250
and plug this directly into
our development infrastructure

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:52.570
and our build system.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:56.770
So now as we start implementing
the algorithms on the device,

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:58.300
we can pull that
data down into the

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:00.240
build system every time
we have a new build

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.990
and make sure the
algorithm is still doing

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:03.362
what we expect it to be doing.

00:11:03.362 --> 00:11:04.820
And this is really
powerful for us,

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:06.528
because now we can do
regression testing.

00:11:06.528 --> 00:11:09.460
We can make sure that as
we develop new features,

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:12.940
we don't go backwards on
any of our other features.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.160
And finally, we can take
that output from our

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:18.560
build system and actually feed
it back into the metadata.

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:21.160
So now we have tags on all of
our data showing which builds

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:24.280
were successful,
which builds weren't.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:26.650
We can start exporting
this data into BigQuery

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:28.370
and really doing
analytics on it.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:31.109
So we can find out,
is there a trend?

00:11:31.109 --> 00:11:31.900
Is there a pattern?

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.330
Is there maybe a
particular algorithm

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:36.580
that has trouble at very
high temperatures or very

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:37.810
low temperatures?

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:40.240
If there's a particular
data set that

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.370
had a bunch of events
all happening at once,

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:45.040
we can see that and
say, oh, maybe we

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:47.524
have a timing problem
on the prototype

00:11:47.524 --> 00:11:48.940
that we're working
on that we need

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:51.220
to dig into and make sure that
our software is addressing

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:51.800
that.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:55.070
And this sort of all can work
because we have a common data

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:57.310
set that's shared by
everybody and we know what

00:11:57.310 --> 00:12:01.459
we're trying to experiment on.

00:12:01.459 --> 00:12:03.250
And so this lets us
build better algorithms

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:04.810
because we have better data.

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:07.750
And more importantly, we
get to spend more time

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:10.360
on collecting the data and
less on all of the record

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:11.860
keeping, because
the service handles

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.140
the record keeping for us.

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:16.450
And as I mentioned
earlier, this whole service

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.060
took one engineer that
didn't have any service

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:20.440
experience at all--

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:23.509
the algorithms team is
mathematicians, roboticists,

00:12:23.509 --> 00:12:24.550
machine learning experts.

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:27.594
It doesn't cover any systems
or services engineers.

00:12:27.594 --> 00:12:30.010
And it was able to be built
over a three day period, which

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:30.801
is really powerful.

00:12:33.067 --> 00:12:34.650
So, now we're at the
point where we've

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:35.820
sort of proven the hardware.

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:37.754
We understand that our
sensors can collect

00:12:37.754 --> 00:12:39.420
the right information
and we can sort of

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:40.590
implement the algorithm.

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:42.500
But algorithms don't
exist in a vacuum.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:44.580
It's actually very important
that we interact with the user

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:45.330
during algorithms.

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:47.460
So we want to understand
how a user is going

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:49.860
to use the different
features on the device

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:53.160
and how they're going
to be affected by it.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.860
And so we need another
service for this

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:57.930
to actually explore
these different features.

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:00.600
And so we have our Nest cloud,
which I've sort of shown here.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:04.920
That sort of interfaces between
your phone or your laptop

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:07.680
and all the different smart
devices that you might have.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.082
But at this sort of phase
of the product development,

00:13:10.082 --> 00:13:11.040
we're still very small.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.500
We're still sort of
trying to figure out

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:14.800
how this feature really works.

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:16.800
And so what we actually
want is a small service,

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:18.800
something that we can
iterate on really quickly.

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:23.350
We can deploy maybe once every
10 minutes, deploy every hour,

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:24.434
things like that.

00:13:24.434 --> 00:13:26.100
And we're just not
ready to integrate it

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:28.260
with our production app
and our production service

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.259
and go through all of the
release processes that

00:13:30.259 --> 00:13:31.482
are needed there.

00:13:31.482 --> 00:13:33.690
And we want to make our
algorithms engineers directly

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:35.370
responsible for the
logic and implementation,

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:37.560
so that if they find
something that's not working,

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:41.800
they can immediately
address it and fix it.

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:44.970
And so again, we can build a
simple real time service in GCP

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:46.090
to do this.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:48.720
So we'll start with App
Engine as our REST API

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:54.060
that lets both the devices and
the apps communicate together.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.532
We'll feed that through
some algorithm logic

00:13:56.532 --> 00:13:57.990
to implement whatever
feature we're

00:13:57.990 --> 00:13:59.910
looking to implement,
and then store

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:03.600
the state of the device
in Cloud Datastore.

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.150
This lets us use Cloud Datastore
as sort of the backing store

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:08.580
that either an app
or a device can query

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:10.304
to figure out what's going on.

00:14:10.304 --> 00:14:12.720
So you might imagine storing
temperature data or something

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:13.952
like that.

00:14:13.952 --> 00:14:15.660
And again, we can
export this to BigQuery

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:17.914
to let us do analytics on
how a user is interacting

00:14:17.914 --> 00:14:18.580
with the device.

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:22.590
So we can store a record of
did the temperature get changed

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:25.200
by the app or by the
device, and which

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:26.460
direction is things flowing?

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:27.930
Do people prefer
to use the device

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:29.670
to change the temperature or
do they prefer to use the app

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:30.780
to change its temperature?

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:32.238
And we're fortunate
at Nest to have

00:14:32.238 --> 00:14:35.340
a very active internal test
group so we can actually

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:37.770
get quite a good set
of data from just

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:42.007
all of our internal employees
trying things out for us.

00:14:42.007 --> 00:14:44.590
So I want to walk through just
a little bit of how simple this

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:47.666
actually is to build by
showing a bit of code here.

00:14:47.666 --> 00:14:50.290
So the first thing we wanted was
real time state communication.

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:51.748
So we want the apps
and the devices

00:14:51.748 --> 00:14:52.870
to communicate together.

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:55.030
And this we'll do
with App Engine,

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:57.670
and specifically in Python
here with using the web

00:14:57.670 --> 00:15:01.340
app to web framework.

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:04.214
And so we just need
two functions here,

00:15:04.214 --> 00:15:05.630
a get function and
a post function

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:09.230
so that we can update the
state and we can fetch it back.

00:15:09.230 --> 00:15:12.680
And then we use Cloud Datastore
to actually store the data,

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:14.540
sort of do all the
heavy lifting for us.

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:17.330
And the APIs that
Google Cloud provides

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:20.210
are really simple here.

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:22.640
You can see in the
get function, we just

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:26.030
create a key using the
device ID and fetch the data

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:28.950
and write it back out to
whoever called that function.

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:31.700
And then for sort of
similarly in reverse,

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:34.070
we write the data directly
into Cloud Datastore.

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:36.740
Cloud Datastore is NoSQL, so
we can add or remove fields

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:39.284
as we need.

00:15:39.284 --> 00:15:40.700
And it's very easy
to update this.

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:44.390
And it really is just
these 10 lines of code

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:47.120
that let us do this.

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:50.210
And so the next step we want is
using BigQuery for analytics.

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:53.990
This lets us sort of track
how things change over time.

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:55.940
And the BigQuery
streaming API lets

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:58.010
us really quickly send
data over to BigQuery

00:15:58.010 --> 00:15:59.460
and make it
immediately available.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:03.320
So now we can very quickly sort
of look at things in real time

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.200
and don't have to wait to do
our analytics the day after.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:09.189
We can iterate much faster,
as Sam was talking about.

00:16:09.189 --> 00:16:11.480
And again, because all of
this is sort of an integrated

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:14.360
part of GCP, it's very quick.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:16.160
Getting credentials
for authentication

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:18.170
is just a couple of
lines at the top.

00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:19.220
That means that we
don't have to worry

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:21.344
about implementing our own
authentication mechanism

00:16:21.344 --> 00:16:22.830
or anything like that.

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:27.020
And we can be guaranteed
that it's a secure system.

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:30.560
We can know that only this App
Engine application is allowed

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:33.830
to talk to this BigQuery
table to send data over to it,

00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:38.684
and not worry about
configuring it ourselves.

00:16:38.684 --> 00:16:40.100
And then again at
the bottom, it's

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:42.890
just a very simple
API call using

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:46.200
the libraries BigQuery
provides to send data over

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:49.150
to that service.

00:16:49.150 --> 00:16:50.990
And then finally we
can use this service

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:52.640
to explore higher level logic.

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:56.270
So instead of just
persisting and logging state,

00:16:56.270 --> 00:16:58.160
we can actually implement
this complex logic.

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:01.160
And so here I've sort of
put a function in the middle

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:02.870
that I've labeled
apply_logic, which

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:04.490
sort of sounds like cheating.

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:07.579
But it really isn't because
all it is a Python function.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:10.220
And that's the magic is
that we don't have to do

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:11.780
a whole lot of extra work.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:13.430
We just write a
Python function that

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:16.069
takes some existing
state and new state,

00:17:16.069 --> 00:17:19.369
and does some arithmetic or
does some logic and figures

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:20.660
out what we want to do.

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:22.500
And this lets us
iterate, again, very,

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:24.922
very quickly, so that if we
want to try something out,

00:17:24.922 --> 00:17:27.380
we don't have to go through
the process of creating a build

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:29.270
for the device, validating
that that build's

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:31.430
not going to break
the device, and then

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:35.965
doing a rolling software
update on our test devices.

00:17:35.965 --> 00:17:37.340
We can actually
just very quickly

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:40.490
change something in the service,
redeploy, see how it's going,

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:42.260
and keep doing that
over and over until we

00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:45.064
have something that works well.

00:17:45.064 --> 00:17:46.730
There are a bunch of
other useful things

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:48.170
that are just sort
of hidden away

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:50.840
in App Engine that
make developing pretty

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:52.460
easy or pretty remarkable.

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:54.650
And one of these is
the deferred library

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:55.850
that I've highlighted here.

00:17:55.850 --> 00:18:00.020
So this lets you schedule
things to happen later,

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:02.480
by taking advantage of
the App Engine task queue.

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:04.670
And so here I'm deferring
the BigQuery insert,

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:09.020
because that can sometimes
be an operation that maybe

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:10.380
takes a little bit of time.

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:12.421
And so we don't want to
put that in the real time

00:18:12.421 --> 00:18:14.180
loop of devices fetching state.

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:16.490
And so we just do that
sort of asynchronously.

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:18.800
And then additionally,
I've written

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:20.000
this to schedule a callback.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:23.800
So in 300 seconds, that
callback function will execute.

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:25.550
And maybe we can make
a different decision

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:27.110
that we made up
front, or reconsider

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:29.090
the decision we made initially.

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:31.370
And that again is a
very powerful thing

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:32.090
to be able to do.

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:33.965
And it's even more
powerful that we can do it

00:18:33.965 --> 00:18:37.320
with a single line of code.

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:40.080
And all of this sort
of has the function

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:41.730
of making services easy for us.

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:44.160
So everything just
sort of scales.

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:46.350
Datastore, BigQuery, App
Engine are all things

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:48.150
that just scale by
default. And so we

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:51.690
don't have to worry when our
little 10 devices that we were

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:54.420
testing on all of a sudden
becomes the whole company

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:56.497
trying out our product.

00:18:56.497 --> 00:18:58.080
You never want to
be in that situation

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:00.480
where you have to tell
someone, oh, I just

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:04.556
built this on my desktop and I
can't let anybody else use it.

00:19:04.556 --> 00:19:06.930
Now we can make sure that
everything that we're building,

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:10.290
anybody in the company that
needs to use it can use it.

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:12.230
We don't have to spend
any time managing VMs.

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:15.327
Again, not a services engineer,
not a systems engineer.

00:19:15.327 --> 00:19:17.910
I don't want to make sure that
I've closed all the right ports

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:19.692
on some machine somewhere.

00:19:19.692 --> 00:19:21.150
Because App Engine's
the interface,

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:24.255
it's sort of secure by
default. And the built in OAuth

00:19:24.255 --> 00:19:26.700
authentication lets us use
all of the Google services

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:30.000
and make sure that those
services are secure

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.230
and that the only data that--

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:38.090
the data can only
be accessed by us.

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:40.590
And really we get to focus on
the logic of the applications,

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:42.180
which are the bits
we're good at,

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:44.160
and let Google focus on
the mechanics, which is

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:47.014
the bits they're very good at.

00:19:47.014 --> 00:19:48.930
And then from there, I'm
going to turn it over

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:51.690
to Peter who's going to tell you
about how we scale all this out

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:54.654
and monitor it in production.

00:19:54.654 --> 00:19:56.850
PETER GRABOWSKI: So hi, my
name is Peter Grabowski.

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:59.100
I'm the tech lead and manager
of the data integration

00:19:59.100 --> 00:19:59.910
team at Nest.

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:02.730
I wanted to start off by saying
thank you to Sam for giving us

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:05.070
a great overview of the
managed technologies

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:06.690
available within
Google, and a thank

00:20:06.690 --> 00:20:09.930
you to Kevin for a
great walkthrough of how

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:14.770
his team develops and
prototypes algorithms at Nest.

00:20:14.770 --> 00:20:18.790
So I'd like to talk to you today
a little bit about how we think

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:21.640
about scaling out those
algorithms, the systems that

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:25.060
make them scale, and also
once they have been deployed,

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:27.670
how we monitor them and
make sure they do what

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:29.170
we think that they're doing.

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:31.780
So this is our architecture.

00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:34.030
To get started, I'd like
to give a quick tour

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:35.440
through our architecture.

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:37.660
This won't be the focal
point of the talk,

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:40.150
but it will come in handy and
provide some useful context

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:42.040
for later in our discussion.

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:44.290
One of the things I wanted
to highlight right away

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:47.110
is that, as Sam mentioned,
we've got both open source

00:20:47.110 --> 00:20:48.730
and managed technologies here.

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:51.040
And being able to
co-locate those gracefully

00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:55.120
within the same environment
was a major draw for using GCP.

00:20:55.120 --> 00:20:57.070
So you can see some
managed technologies

00:20:57.070 --> 00:20:58.270
that Sam talked about.

00:20:58.270 --> 00:21:00.070
You've got Dataflow
and BigQuery.

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:02.110
You can also see some
open source technologies

00:21:02.110 --> 00:21:07.770
that we use, like Apache Storm,
Apache Kafka, and Hadoop HDFS.

00:21:07.770 --> 00:21:10.440
So our system begins,
like many others,

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:11.880
with the data logging service.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:13.890
We've got many devices
out in the field.

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:14.700
We've got cameras.

00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:16.230
We've got smoke detectors.

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:17.730
We've got thermostats.

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:19.809
They're all sending
data up to the service.

00:21:19.809 --> 00:21:21.600
They're sending it up
in different formats.

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:23.070
They're sending it up
in different sizes.

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:24.930
They're sending it up
in different shapes.

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:26.610
No matter what size
or shape they're

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:29.910
sending it up in, it's received
by the data logging service.

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:32.010
This sits out in front
of our architecture,

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:33.690
and it's responsible
for three things.

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:36.540
So it's designed to be very
purposely straightforward.

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:37.860
It receives the data.

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:40.560
It authenticates that the
data came from a Nest device,

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:42.420
that it's not someone
trying to spoof us.

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:46.130
And then it writes it
to a durable store.

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:48.530
From there, we move to
the processing layer.

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:51.404
The processing layer is
made up of Storm and Kafka.

00:21:51.404 --> 00:21:52.820
On the Storm side
of things, we've

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:56.180
got one topology that is
responsible for each kind

00:21:56.180 --> 00:21:58.010
of data that we process at Nest.

00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:00.950
But no matter which
topology it is, no matter

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:04.610
which data source it is, that
topologies do the same thing.

00:22:04.610 --> 00:22:07.250
They extract the data from
whatever format it's in.

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:08.900
They transform it
into avro, and then

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:12.280
they load that data into Kafka.

00:22:12.280 --> 00:22:14.490
From there, we move
to the loading layer.

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:16.839
The loading data consists
of Cloud Dataflow.

00:22:16.839 --> 00:22:19.130
And I want to highlight one
of the really nice features

00:22:19.130 --> 00:22:21.550
of Cloud Dataflow, which
is, as Sam mentioned,

00:22:21.550 --> 00:22:23.560
it can run in both batch
and streaming mode.

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:25.570
So this might sound
like a simple thing,

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:27.730
but it ends up making
our operational workflows

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:28.750
a lot easier.

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:30.910
We can use streaming
mode for streaming data

00:22:30.910 --> 00:22:33.310
in real time for the normal
operation of the service.

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:35.590
But we can also use batch
mode by just changing

00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:38.290
a few lines of
configuration to do

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:41.200
historical back loads or
particularly large load jobs.

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:45.200
So this simple feature ends
up saving us a ton of time.

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:48.140
From there, Dataflow writes
into our storage layer.

00:22:48.140 --> 00:22:50.660
We primarily use BigQuery,
although as I mentioned before,

00:22:50.660 --> 00:22:54.860
we also maintain a footprint
in Bigtable and HDFS.

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:57.680
And so up into this point,
what we've seen so far

00:22:57.680 --> 00:22:59.557
constitutes our data platform.

00:22:59.557 --> 00:23:01.640
Everything else that we're
going to talk about now

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:04.280
constitutes the
applications, or applications

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:05.870
that run on top of the platform.

00:23:05.870 --> 00:23:07.814
We've got a number of
different applications.

00:23:07.814 --> 00:23:09.980
We've got some monitoring
applications and reporting

00:23:09.980 --> 00:23:12.692
applications that help us make
sure our devices are doing

00:23:12.692 --> 00:23:14.150
what we think they
should be doing,

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:17.210
and also that are general
fleet health is strong.

00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:19.190
We've got machine
learning algorithms

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:20.690
that come from
Kevin and his team

00:23:20.690 --> 00:23:23.540
that we've scaled up
to run on production.

00:23:23.540 --> 00:23:25.700
We've also got a number
of analytics applications

00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:28.620
or various workflows from
our consumer analytics teams.

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:30.290
And finally, a
number of consumer

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:32.360
insights that we
take and transform

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:36.500
the data from the devices back
into value for our customers.

00:23:36.500 --> 00:23:38.690
I'll talk about two my
favorite consumer insights

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:42.121
application later on
in the presentation.

00:23:42.121 --> 00:23:44.620
So at this point, we're at a
point that I'm sure many of you

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:45.700
have been at before.

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:46.960
We've got all the data loaded.

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:48.820
We've got all the data stored.

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:50.530
Now what do we do with it?

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:52.065
So we had the same question.

00:23:52.065 --> 00:23:54.190
We went out and talked to
all of our device teams--

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:55.898
the camera team, the
smoke detector team,

00:23:55.898 --> 00:23:58.120
the thermostat team, all
of the algorithms teams

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:00.430
to see what they might
like to do with it,

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:02.020
all of the analytics
teams to see

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:03.460
how they might like to use it.

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:05.170
And no matter the team
that we talked to,

00:24:05.170 --> 00:24:07.090
the results came back the same.

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:10.000
They wanted to be able to do
fleet level analysis quickly

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:11.210
and easily.

00:24:11.210 --> 00:24:13.600
So to help them out, we
built our first prototype.

00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:16.690
We built it using PySpark and
Zeppelin, two great open source

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:18.430
technologies out of Apache.

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:20.070
It looks something like this.

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:22.760
And I'd like to take a
minute to walk through it.

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:24.150
So this was HVAC runtime.

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:25.870
It's calculated with PySpark.

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:27.550
For those of you
who aren't familiar,

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:29.530
HVAC stands for heating,
ventilation, and air

00:24:29.530 --> 00:24:30.340
conditioning.

00:24:30.340 --> 00:24:32.319
So it's a measure of how
much time your furnace

00:24:32.319 --> 00:24:34.360
or how much time your air
conditioner was running

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:35.950
throughout the course of a day.

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:39.250
You can see on the x-axis here,
we've got fraction of a day

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:40.570
with HVAC running.

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:42.160
This is a histogram.

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:44.050
On the y-axis, you've
got the fraction

00:24:44.050 --> 00:24:45.640
of the device population.

00:24:45.640 --> 00:24:48.430
So all the way on the
right, you see that about 5%

00:24:48.430 --> 00:24:51.610
of the population was
running 100% of the day.

00:24:51.610 --> 00:24:55.540
All the way on the left, you
see about 20% of the population

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:58.630
was running not at all
during the course of the day.

00:24:58.630 --> 00:25:02.110
This might be alarming,
except that this data came

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:03.910
from a set of test devices.

00:25:03.910 --> 00:25:06.010
So that leads into one
of the major complexities

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:08.960
that we found with this system.

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:11.200
It was really costly
to run analyzes

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:12.910
like this on all of production.

00:25:12.910 --> 00:25:14.950
We had the capacity to
run it on production.

00:25:14.950 --> 00:25:16.870
And for the most important
workflows we did.

00:25:16.870 --> 00:25:20.320
But if you wanted to think
about other types of workflows,

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:22.930
it became really
tricky to do so.

00:25:22.930 --> 00:25:24.790
One of the main reasons
this was so tricky

00:25:24.790 --> 00:25:27.220
is that our storage was
coupled to our compute.

00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:30.940
So unless we picked exactly
the right instance type

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:32.800
with exactly the
right amount of RAM,

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:34.450
exactly the right
amount of storage,

00:25:34.450 --> 00:25:36.550
and exactly the
right number of CPUs,

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:39.760
we always had too much of one
and not enough of the other.

00:25:39.760 --> 00:25:41.620
I was describing this
problem to my roommate

00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:43.240
as I was preparing
this talk, and she

00:25:43.240 --> 00:25:47.012
mentioned that she had the same
problem, except for she had it

00:25:47.012 --> 00:25:48.970
when she went to the
store and she bought chips

00:25:48.970 --> 00:25:50.080
and she bought salsa.

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:52.240
So no matter what, she
always said too much of one

00:25:52.240 --> 00:25:53.590
and not enough for the other.

00:25:53.590 --> 00:25:55.630
We've got the same
problem at Nest,

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:58.450
except for we've got it
with storage and compute.

00:25:58.450 --> 00:26:02.050
Of course that leads to
frequent resource contention.

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:05.140
We had when cluster where
the data were located.

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:07.570
We ran both high
priority workflows on it,

00:26:07.570 --> 00:26:09.880
but also lower priority
analytics tasks,

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:11.800
which meant that the
lower priority analytics

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:15.550
tasks were frequently preempted
or bumped from the workflows.

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:17.170
This meant that
our analytics team

00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:19.750
had to either go back
and reschedule or rerun

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:23.290
those workflows, or have
gaps in their analysis.

00:26:23.290 --> 00:26:27.060
That also meant that our team
took on the role of a part time

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:27.560
sysadmin.

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:32.602
We got many phone
calls saying, hey,

00:26:32.602 --> 00:26:34.060
this workflow has
just been killed.

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:35.230
It's run out of memory.

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:37.600
Can you help us get
it running again,

00:26:37.600 --> 00:26:41.222
which wasn't a great working
environment for the team.

00:26:41.222 --> 00:26:43.180
There were a number of
operational complexities

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:44.600
that we ran into as well.

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:47.230
So PySpark and Zeppelin
were great tools,

00:26:47.230 --> 00:26:50.120
but they weren't built to do
this sort of dashboarding.

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:52.210
So we ended up building
a separate service that

00:26:52.210 --> 00:26:55.990
was responsible for generating
JSON blobs that represented

00:26:55.990 --> 00:26:57.940
these templates that
we wanted to create,

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:01.060
loading them into the notebook
cache of the Zeppelin monitor,

00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:03.850
restarting the service so the
Zeppelin monitor picked up

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:05.147
on the new notebooks.

00:27:05.147 --> 00:27:06.730
This worked well for
one user, but you

00:27:06.730 --> 00:27:08.312
can imagine as
multiple users started

00:27:08.312 --> 00:27:10.270
trying to use this system
and it was frequently

00:27:10.270 --> 00:27:12.490
restarting all the time, it
wasn't a great experience

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:13.370
for them.

00:27:13.370 --> 00:27:14.830
Additionally there
was a constraint

00:27:14.830 --> 00:27:17.246
that there was a maximum number
of notebooks that could be

00:27:17.246 --> 00:27:18.864
loaded on the machine at once.

00:27:18.864 --> 00:27:20.530
So we had to build a
second service that

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:22.690
was responsible for
backing these notebooks up

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:24.520
to durable store and
then pulling them back

00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:26.050
in when we needed them.

00:27:26.050 --> 00:27:28.390
It wasn't a great
experience for the people

00:27:28.390 --> 00:27:30.550
who wanted to see the data.

00:27:30.550 --> 00:27:32.610
This next point is an
especially important one.

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:35.610
I want to take a moment
to talk about it.

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:37.680
One of the things that we
care most about at Nest

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:40.050
is protecting our users'
data and protecting

00:27:40.050 --> 00:27:41.250
our users' privacy.

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:44.340
And many of the mechanisms from
the systems that we were using

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:46.200
provided only clunky
authentication

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:48.870
and authorization, which
made it difficult to do

00:27:48.870 --> 00:27:51.090
fast and flexible data
analysis to return value

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:52.750
to our customers.

00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:55.110
A similar problem
was that it became

00:27:55.110 --> 00:27:58.510
very difficult to build analyzes
back to different teams.

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:02.430
So for example, imagine that
the thermostat team came to us

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:04.920
and said, hey, we've got
this great new data type

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:06.810
that we want to store.

00:28:06.810 --> 00:28:08.569
We know that your
cluster is full.

00:28:08.569 --> 00:28:10.860
We know that we're going to
have to expand the cluster.

00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:15.060
What's it going to take
to load that data type in?

00:28:15.060 --> 00:28:17.430
It was difficult to figure
out exactly how much

00:28:17.430 --> 00:28:19.750
that extra space would cost.

00:28:19.750 --> 00:28:22.380
We had some heuristics to help
us come up with estimates.

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:24.750
They always proved to either
be too high or too low.

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:27.000
And so at the end we ended
up billing most of the cost

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:29.130
back to our team, which
was great from a billings

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:31.350
perspective or a
management perspective,

00:28:31.350 --> 00:28:34.000
but terrible from a team
bottom line perspective.

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:37.410
And finally, if you were an
analytics team that had already

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:39.600
generated some data
that you wanted

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:42.150
to use to augment or
color your analysis,

00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:44.400
it was difficult to get
data into the system.

00:28:44.400 --> 00:28:45.900
You had to learn a
new set of tools.

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:47.760
You had to get the
appropriate permissions.

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:49.884
And then you had to figure
out how to join and link

00:28:49.884 --> 00:28:51.140
those two together.

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:52.950
So I'd like to take
a little bit of time

00:28:52.950 --> 00:28:55.110
to talk about the solution
that we came up with.

00:28:55.110 --> 00:28:57.180
So on the left here,
this is a zoomed

00:28:57.180 --> 00:29:00.480
in view of the architecture
that we discussed previously.

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:03.690
And it starts with the events
in Cloud BigQuery up at the top.

00:29:03.690 --> 00:29:05.550
So those events
are the same events

00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:09.170
that were loaded from Kafka
into BigQuery by Dataflow.

00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:12.120
And once we've got the
data available in BigQuery,

00:29:12.120 --> 00:29:14.800
we then built a processing
library on top of it.

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:16.860
The processing library
was responsible for two

00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:17.920
different pieces.

00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:20.400
One, there's a number
of Nest-specific nuances

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:21.460
in our data set.

00:29:21.460 --> 00:29:24.420
So the processing library
was aware of those nuances

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:26.670
and provided a higher level
abstraction on top of them

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:28.380
to make it easier to work with.

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:30.600
And two, one of the
most frequent kinds

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:33.310
of analyzes that we do at
Nest is time series analysis.

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:36.450
So it provided a number of
very useful higher level

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:38.820
abstractions for
time series analysis.

00:29:38.820 --> 00:29:40.890
One of the really nice
things, as Sam mentioned,

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:44.040
was that even though we're
running in Cloud Dataflow,

00:29:44.040 --> 00:29:45.660
the code is just Apache Beam.

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:47.940
It's completely open
source, completely portable.

00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:51.960
So if we wanted to, we could
pull this code out of Dataflow

00:29:51.960 --> 00:29:54.090
and run it elsewhere, run
it on our own cluster,

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:56.106
run it wherever
we might want to.

00:29:56.106 --> 00:29:57.480
We're not planning
on doing that.

00:29:57.480 --> 00:29:58.830
We love working with Dataflow.

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:02.400
But it is really nice to have
the flexibility to do that.

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:04.980
Wherever we do the computation,
after it's complete,

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:07.980
we load the data back into
BigQuery for further processing

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:08.882
or analysis.

00:30:08.882 --> 00:30:11.340
And from there there's a number
of really nice integrations

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:13.290
that make it easy to visualize.

00:30:13.290 --> 00:30:15.030
Two of my favorites
are up on the screen.

00:30:15.030 --> 00:30:17.160
You've got Data
Studio and Datalab.

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:20.889
But it's also got really easy
connectors for Excel or Tableau

00:30:20.889 --> 00:30:22.680
or Google Sheets or
whatever you might want

00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:26.020
to use to take a look at it.

00:30:26.020 --> 00:30:28.580
So I'd like to talk
about two advantages,

00:30:28.580 --> 00:30:31.240
or a couple of advantages
from using the system.

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:33.610
First and foremost
was autoscaling.

00:30:33.610 --> 00:30:35.680
This autoscaling
was huge for us.

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:38.290
It made it really easy for
us to develop code locally,

00:30:38.290 --> 00:30:40.540
to test code locally,
and then to push it out

00:30:40.540 --> 00:30:43.030
to our production cluster
to run at scale by only

00:30:43.030 --> 00:30:44.622
changing a few configs.

00:30:44.622 --> 00:30:46.330
And to demonstrate
how powerful this was,

00:30:46.330 --> 00:30:47.800
I'd like to tell a short story.

00:30:47.800 --> 00:30:50.380
We had a camera systems engineer
who was a great engineer,

00:30:50.380 --> 00:30:52.450
but again didn't have
too much familiarity

00:30:52.450 --> 00:30:55.000
with building services
or analytics workflows.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:57.640
He was able to get up and
running using the libraries

00:30:57.640 --> 00:30:59.560
we created in only a day.

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:01.480
So there was a huge
operational advantage

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:03.250
from using this system.

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:05.170
Part of the reason
it was such a big win

00:31:05.170 --> 00:31:09.250
was we were finally able to
decouple storage from compute.

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:11.680
So as I mentioned before,
all of this storage

00:31:11.680 --> 00:31:13.120
was handled by BigQuery.

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:15.550
All of the compute was
handled by Dataflow.

00:31:15.550 --> 00:31:17.830
So we can add new
logs to the system

00:31:17.830 --> 00:31:20.740
without worrying about
scaling out Dataflow,

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:23.350
or we could add new
analysis pipelines

00:31:23.350 --> 00:31:26.440
without worrying about
scaling out BigQuery.

00:31:26.440 --> 00:31:29.249
And finally, because everything
runs as a managed service,

00:31:29.249 --> 00:31:30.790
there was no more
resource contention

00:31:30.790 --> 00:31:33.400
with production jobs, which
meant that the analytics

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:38.050
teams could run their
jobs without worrying

00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:41.920
about backfill, and also
without worrying about missing

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:45.160
windows in their data sets,
and that our team got many

00:31:45.160 --> 00:31:47.540
fewer late night phone calls.

00:31:47.540 --> 00:31:49.750
There are a number of
operational advantages as well.

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:51.190
This first one is a big one.

00:31:51.190 --> 00:31:54.100
They've got this really
nicely defined access control

00:31:54.100 --> 00:31:57.610
model that made it really easy
for us to grant people access

00:31:57.610 --> 00:31:59.560
to the right data at
the right time and only

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:00.560
when they need it.

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:02.800
So their access control
model was great and increased

00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:04.270
our operating security.

00:32:04.270 --> 00:32:10.250
A similar system, we're finally
able to build analyzes by team.

00:32:10.250 --> 00:32:11.770
So whenever you
kick off a workflow,

00:32:11.770 --> 00:32:15.220
you're able to set a billing
config or billing ID.

00:32:15.220 --> 00:32:17.920
That means that the teams
that kick off workflows

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:20.020
are billed for only the
compute cycles they use

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:21.160
and nothing more.

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:23.050
And finally as we
mentioned previously,

00:32:23.050 --> 00:32:25.370
really great options
for data visualization.

00:32:25.370 --> 00:32:27.040
Data Studio, Datalab,
Tableau, anything

00:32:27.040 --> 00:32:30.120
that you want to work
with is available.

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:32.390
So I'd like to revisit one
of the example use cases

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:33.590
that we talked about before.

00:32:33.590 --> 00:32:36.500
This is the same HVAC runtime
analysis, except for this time

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:38.400
it's computed with
Cloud Dataflow.

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:40.730
So on the x-axis you
see the day that it ran.

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:42.830
On the y-axis you see
the fraction of time

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:45.320
that your furnace or
air conditioner was on.

00:32:45.320 --> 00:32:47.960
It starts out low in October
while things are still

00:32:47.960 --> 00:32:48.830
pretty warm.

00:32:48.830 --> 00:32:51.140
It increases through
January 10th where there was

00:32:51.140 --> 00:32:52.850
a huge storm in the Northeast.

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:54.350
And then it falls
back off as things

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:56.280
start to warm up in the spring.

00:32:56.280 --> 00:32:58.500
This is a really
simple computation.

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:01.137
But this graph hides a
really important fact.

00:33:01.137 --> 00:33:02.720
This is one of the
first times that we

00:33:02.720 --> 00:33:05.760
were able to run an analysis
like this on all of production.

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:08.234
So all of those sampling
pieces that I talked before,

00:33:08.234 --> 00:33:09.650
where you're worried
about getting

00:33:09.650 --> 00:33:12.320
the wrong population or
missing population or masking

00:33:12.320 --> 00:33:14.780
populations, we don't
have to worry about.

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:17.376
This is great if you want to
build more complicated or more

00:33:17.376 --> 00:33:19.500
nuanced machine learning
models on top of the data.

00:33:19.500 --> 00:33:23.810
You can be sure that you're
hitting all of the points.

00:33:23.810 --> 00:33:26.150
So with that, I
want to highlight

00:33:26.150 --> 00:33:29.000
that even a simple computation
like the one that I just

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:32.150
mentioned, even just figuring
out HVAC on and off periods

00:33:32.150 --> 00:33:34.670
can have some really
positive returns and value

00:33:34.670 --> 00:33:35.900
for our customers.

00:33:35.900 --> 00:33:38.960
This is one of my favorite
products that we offer at Nest.

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:41.400
This is the monthly
energy report.

00:33:41.400 --> 00:33:43.430
So every month we
email our Nest users

00:33:43.430 --> 00:33:48.045
and say how much energy you
used as compared to last month.

00:33:48.045 --> 00:33:50.420
We try to sprinkle a little
machine learning on top of it

00:33:50.420 --> 00:33:53.210
to say why your
energy usage changed.

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:54.770
Maybe it got colder.

00:33:54.770 --> 00:33:55.964
Maybe the weather changed.

00:33:55.964 --> 00:33:57.380
Maybe you adjusted
your thermostat

00:33:57.380 --> 00:33:59.330
to be more comfortable
in your home.

00:33:59.330 --> 00:34:02.690
We also provide one of my
favorite statistics at Nest.

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:07.070
So every month we give a summary
of how much energy people have

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:10.721
saved by installing Nest
across the entire population.

00:34:10.721 --> 00:34:11.929
This is the one from January.

00:34:11.929 --> 00:34:14.389
So this is current
through January 31.

00:34:14.389 --> 00:34:16.310
Since October 11th--

00:34:16.310 --> 00:34:19.010
I'm sorry, October
2011, Nesters have

00:34:19.010 --> 00:34:23.030
saved 11.3 billion kilowatt
hours, which is enough energy

00:34:23.030 --> 00:34:25.699
to freeze all of the ice
necessary for 2 million ice

00:34:25.699 --> 00:34:26.659
skating rinks.

00:34:26.659 --> 00:34:28.940
So that's a pretty
cool insight that

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:31.489
comes out of a relatively
straightforward data analysis

00:34:31.489 --> 00:34:34.280
once you're able to perform
it on the entire fleet.

00:34:34.280 --> 00:34:37.070
It's also a lot of
energy to save simply

00:34:37.070 --> 00:34:40.070
by installing a thermostat.

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:42.290
Fast and flexible
data analysis also

00:34:42.290 --> 00:34:45.170
lets us prove how
efficient our devices are.

00:34:45.170 --> 00:34:46.670
So we're really
proud to announce

00:34:46.670 --> 00:34:49.190
that Nest was the first
thermostat in America

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:51.730
with an Energy
Star certification.

00:34:51.730 --> 00:34:53.840
And some of the fast and
flexible data analysis

00:34:53.840 --> 00:34:55.389
techniques that I
talked about helped

00:34:55.389 --> 00:34:59.130
us be able to prove
this efficiency out.

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:01.310
So with that, I'd
like to wrap up.

00:35:01.310 --> 00:35:04.820
Using the technologies
available to us within GCP

00:35:04.820 --> 00:35:07.190
provided an easy
path from development

00:35:07.190 --> 00:35:10.910
to production, paved by
shared and scalable tooling

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:11.690
throughout.

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:14.870
So that means that an
algorithms engineer like Kevin

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:18.650
can develop and prototype data
sets using one set of tools,

00:35:18.650 --> 00:35:21.860
and then take those same tools
to do analysis when he deploys

00:35:21.860 --> 00:35:23.450
the algorithm to production.

00:35:23.450 --> 00:35:26.960
There's no need to learn another
toolset for doing data at scale

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:30.860
or for doing smaller
data analysis locally.

00:35:30.860 --> 00:35:34.400
The access control models they
provide give the right people

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:37.280
the right access to the
right data at the right time.

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:39.921
And similarly, the billing
operations that they allow for

00:35:39.921 --> 00:35:42.170
allows you to build the right
teams for what they use,

00:35:42.170 --> 00:35:45.710
and more importantly,
for only what they use.

00:35:45.710 --> 00:35:48.400
But most importantly,
the data availability

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:51.010
and access controls
provided by GCP

00:35:51.010 --> 00:35:54.640
help us further our mission of
creating the home that cares.

00:35:54.640 --> 00:35:56.200
So with that, I'd
like to thank you

00:35:56.200 --> 00:35:58.300
to all of the team
members at Nest and Google

00:35:58.300 --> 00:36:00.240
who contributed
to these projects.

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:01.870
These weren't built
by a single person.

00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:05.500
We have a great engineering
team at Nest, seven of whom

00:36:05.500 --> 00:36:07.835
are here today that are
wonderful to work with.

00:36:07.835 --> 00:36:09.710
We've got a great
engineering team at Google,

00:36:09.710 --> 00:36:11.584
including people like
Sam, who have helped us

00:36:11.584 --> 00:36:12.580
along the process.

00:36:12.580 --> 00:36:14.840
So thank you to both.

00:36:14.840 --> 00:36:17.990
And I'd also like to thank you
in the audience for staying

00:36:17.990 --> 00:36:18.740
and listening.

00:36:18.740 --> 00:36:20.300
We'll be outside after the talk.

00:36:20.300 --> 00:36:23.090
Feel free to come up, ask any
questions that you might have,

00:36:23.090 --> 00:36:23.670
say hello.

00:36:23.670 --> 00:36:26.660
And like I mentioned, we've also
got some other data services

00:36:26.660 --> 00:36:28.910
engineers from Nest who
can help to field questions

00:36:28.910 --> 00:36:31.080
or provide more information.

00:36:31.080 --> 00:36:31.910
So thank you.

00:36:31.910 --> 00:36:33.710
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:33.710 --> 00:36:37.060
